          {
            "id": "from-rank-discrepancy-to-thesis",
            "text": "A remaining discrepancy shaped the presentation's central claim. The presenter observed that Claude could receive comparable absolute scores while winning less often than the other systems and asked for a more nuanced account rather than declaring the evaluation flawed. The analysis found a presentation-position effect: two judges favored the first candidate while another tended toward later positions. Balanced randomization made a complete rank reversal unlikely, but the GLM–Claude writing-style near-tie was not treated as precise to fractions of a point. The direct audit was consequently framed as a lower-assumption diagnostic for whether structured evaluators recover system differences, not as ground truth or an automatic replacement score.",
