              "eventId": "evt-2fbb820162ce668b53d70bd19eed77d6630e5766866f5219a6ec2a62d34f7c18",
              "label": "Question about randomized-order correction and raw source answers"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "audit-design-agent",
          "releaseLabel": "Audit design agent",
          "role": "Protocol designer and analyst",
          "description": "Translated the research questions into an anonymous, order-corrected audit; implemented its bounded manifests; monitored execution; and reported results with explicit caveats.",
          "localIdentityState": "not_identified",
          "evidence": [
            {
              "documentId": "traj-zxiong-codex-c4c6af085497b69d",
              "eventId": "evt-0ccb459c10d9bf3a0ab74b4b225d5e7a62e30162e3905d0177f44ee21c54d573",
              "label": "Initial protocol decisions and canonical recoding requirement"
            },
            {
              "documentId": "traj-zxiong-codex-c4c6af085497b69d",
              "eventId": "evt-a3618a0ce9185480ba625966a80ef5ba8f231cd0dc17ad1a8e0c77242e338ae8",
              "label": "Final context boundary and shared protocol specification"
            }
          ]
        }
      ],
      "story": {
        "blocks": [
          {
            "id": "diagnostic-purpose",
            "text": "The audit began with a narrow validity question rather than a plan to replace the existing evaluators. Its role was to provide a lower-assumption behavioral diagnostic: did structured HUMANUAL and SimulatorArena scores recover the same system differences as direct judgments about target-user specificity and human/source indistinguishability? Existing evaluator scores remained predictions to be validated, not ground truth. The design also excluded the structurally different HUMANUAL chat subset instead of silently mixing it into a profile-based analysis.",
            "evidence": [
              {
                "documentId": "traj-zxiong-codex-c4c6af085497b69d",
                "eventId": "evt-21cc64f198770fe6864bec378e77e8c9120f3c0cd19ad7d8f53499a2ffff21f5",
                "label": "HUMANUAL chat exclusion and score-direction issue"
              },
              {
                "documentId": "traj-zxiong-codex-c4c6af085497b69d",
                "eventId": "evt-0ccb459c10d9bf3a0ab74b4b225d5e7a62e30162e3905d0177f44ee21c54d573",
                "label": "Audit positioned as diagnostic validation"
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "id": "anonymity-and-order-design",
            "text": "The protocol was built around hidden identity and canonical direction. Judges saw only First and Second, answered on an A–E scale, and never received model names or target, comparison, real, or simulated labels. Hidden post-processing flipped reversed presentations onto fixed target-oriented and source-oriented scales. The evaluator received the substantive profile, history, and task context available to the simulator through a neutral template, while simulator-only role instructions, answer keys, reference outputs, assistant text, and identifying metadata were removed. Specificity and source judgments were isolated into independent items so repeated profiles or candidates inside one prompt could not reveal the intended answer.",
            "evidence": [
              {
                "documentId": "traj-zxiong-codex-c4c6af085497b69d",
                "eventId": "evt-2806d6cea139eea5102646d294bf47800e48d381fb578d3d660d177185eff68d",
                "label": "Anonymous A–E judgments and hidden canonical recoding"
              },
              {
                "documentId": "traj-zxiong-codex-c4c6af085497b69d",
                "eventId": "evt-ce61f841d3e6d5f8df7187bf643d0673cfeaa37cf6702bed5b841b28f1110848",
                "label": "Independent-item batching and source-condition isolation"
              },
              {
                "documentId": "traj-zxiong-codex-c4c6af085497b69d",
                "eventId": "evt-a3618a0ce9185480ba625966a80ef5ba8f231cd0dc17ad1a8e0c77242e338ae8",
                "label": "Exact task-context boundary"
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "id": "scope-revision-and-smoke-boundary",
            "text": "Scope decisions prevented the audit from claiming unsupported longitudinal behavior. The chosen targets were the existing 100 rows from five HUMANUAL subsets and two SimulatorArena subsets, with Sol, GLM, and Claude trajectories reused and DeepSeek excluded. Profiles came from source datasets rather than generated summaries, and no per-user metric was used where repeated observations were insufficient. The smoke design froze same-dataset, non-target comparison histories and separate history-conditioned and context-only source judgments. Nine protocol and integration tests passed, both visible orders were exact reversals, and leak checks found no known model, worker, username, or source identifiers. Execution nevertheless stopped at the submission boundary when the Slurm controller was down; no jobs were created or duplicated.",
            "evidence": [
              {
                "documentId": "traj-zxiong-codex-c4c6af085497b69d",
                "eventId": "evt-9844227cfdcf651a54b1513169f3024c8f1445cf27a9c6cc7a615d5b8457f67e",
                "label": "Revised 700-target scope and no per-user inference"
              },
              {
                "documentId": "traj-zxiong-codex-c4c6af085497b69d",
                "eventId": "evt-83a7c3d4f372306f20692f95e94a7680a3d697a6733e07077b8cedf1c089d5a6",
                "label": "Validated smoke protocol blocked by controller outage"
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "id": "bounded-full-manifest",
            "text": "The eventual full manifest was narrower than the expensive smoke extrapolation. It retained all 700 targets but used two reused comparison histories per dataset and one deterministic randomized presentation per direct item. The resulting package contained 11,300 logical judgments per judge—5,600 specificity, 4,200 human/source, and 1,500 HUMANUAL naive-alignment items—packed into 1,615 requests. Random placement stayed near 49/51 percent, and identity-leak validation remained clean. This was a documented design change from the earlier five-comparison, both-order smoke protocol rather than an unreported substitution.",
            "evidence": [
              {
                "documentId": "traj-zxiong-codex-c4c6af085497b69d",
                "eventId": "evt-83a7c3d4f372306f20692f95e94a7680a3d697a6733e07077b8cedf1c089d5a6",
                "label": "Five-comparison smoke design and cost estimate"
              },
              {
                "documentId": "traj-zxiong-codex-c4c6af085497b69d",
                "eventId": "evt-f31726d728a61d4f6535b5ad5722c2b4446de529fa8ba2456fdd70f273f5fae9",
                "label": "Validated full 11,300-judgment manifest"
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "id": "execution-with-missing-support",
            "text": "Execution preserved missingness rather than manufacturing completion. Gemini's main pass produced 11,286 of 11,300 judgments; two malformed batches left 14 human/source items unresolved, while specificity and alignment were complete. Aggregation used valid common support with no imputation. Under that judge, GLM and Claude had nearly identical audit gaps and pairwise outcomes, while Sol was slightly behind; all three were poor on human/source indistinguishability, with the real response favored in roughly 82 to 85 percent of judgments. A later four-model comparison kept judge lanes separate and found GLM and Claude effectively tied under both full audit references, with paired uncertainty spanning zero.",
            "evidence": [
              {
                "documentId": "traj-zxiong-codex-c4c6af085497b69d",
                "eventId": "evt-3069f8092c04901b14cde2e714361daa98d483cc0f5af1e488ea296f9f26e672",
                "label": "Gemini completion with 14 unresolved judgments"
              },
              {
                "documentId": "traj-zxiong-codex-c4c6af085497b69d",
                "eventId": "evt-0eb8e6b0dbc11b0ad045d4bad0e604216c01180bc17f037fdd41d86eef732ca0",
                "label": "Common-support aggregate and indistinguishability result"
              },
              {
                "documentId": "traj-zxiong-codex-19fd7078bb29d827",
                "eventId": "evt-5da00ce7688233304b2ced66913ffee79148a8f22cf5fe3d27f6b414b4afc209",
                "label": "Judge-specific matched-support four-model comparison"
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "id": "specificity-is-a-gap-not-correctness",
            "text": "The first interpretation correction concerned the meaning of a low specificity gap. D_spec measures whether real and simulated responses receive similar target-versus-comparison history-association ratings; it does not show that either response was correctly associated with the target. Equal low scores can therefore arise when both responses strongly match the target, when both are indistinguishable, or when both incorrectly favor the comparison user. Raw score distributions added the missing direction: generated responses had higher target-history association than real responses overall, particularly GLM under the Luna judge, which was consistent with possible over-personalization or profile caricature rather than automatically better simulation.",
            "evidence": [
              {
                "documentId": "traj-zxiong-codex-c4c6af085497b69d",
                "eventId": "evt-5c57b4f8461e264efc19d0363c95d308c410cfdcd3f85b54dd09961539eae5c6",
                "label": "Specificity-gap interpretation and signed companion metric"
              },
              {
                "documentId": "traj-zxiong-codex-1200a8ebcfbee55e",
                "eventId": "evt-59c9a46ecf62c0f731b9dad043599524c947b32526e24a2d455243d8b231a6dd",
                "label": "Real-versus-model specificity distribution"
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "id": "source-direction-and-score-resolution",
            "text": "The second correction concerned source detection. Random order was handled by canonical recoding, and presentations were balanced near 50/50, but the normalized source gap used absolute distance from the neutral category. That made confidently identifying the human and confidently mistaking the simulator for the human equally distinguishable. Raw, order-corrected answers showed that both judges almost never selected indistinguishable and usually favored the real response, while still sometimes selecting a simulator with high confidence. A separate resolution check found that D_audit was highly discrete: Gemini placed 59.5 percent of cells exactly at 0.5 and its entire interquartile range collapsed there. Ties reduced resolution, but removing tied comparisons did not materially repair the original HUMANUAL evaluator's near-zero pair-gap relationship.",
            "evidence": [
              {
                "documentId": "traj-zxiong-codex-19fd7078bb29d827",
                "eventId": "evt-2fbb820162ce668b53d70bd19eed77d6630e5766866f5219a6ec2a62d34f7c18",
                "label": "Request to verify order handling and inspect raw source answers"
              },
              {
                "documentId": "traj-zxiong-codex-19fd7078bb29d827",
                "eventId": "evt-5474a6e6286b8f9671dd8899b0511fee09fa6fac0a80ff978b14116430f52cce",
                "label": "Canonical source distribution and directional limitation"
              },
              {
                "documentId": "traj-zxiong-codex-19fd7078bb29d827",
                "eventId": "evt-b6da1f93633ecaf42482e013dc9add490b619779388823283ca6acd758b9446e",
                "label": "Discrete audit-score concentration and tie analysis"
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "id": "component-level-validation",
            "text": "Validation was finally separated by component rather than relying only on the weighted composite. For each simulator pair and matched instance, proxy gaps were compared with audit-quality gaps using Kendall's tau-b, then averaged equally across model pairs, datasets, and the two independent audit references. On HUMANUAL, the original evaluator remained near zero for both specificity and source detection. The naive profile-alignment baseline improved the specificity relationship to 0.090 from 0.014, consistently across all three model pairs, but its source-detection relationship stayed near zero. The audit therefore supported a narrower conclusion: the naive baseline captured some relative target-user specificity, while neither proxy meaningfully predicted which simulator would be harder to distinguish from a human on a particular instance.",
            "evidence": [
              {
                "documentId": "traj-zxiong-codex-19fd7078bb29d827",
                "eventId": "evt-4fc138481bdd1dea1837da1a831fd4819f025ad979e714200780c2cf15d97de4",
                "label": "Request for component-specific pair-gap tau-b"
              },
              {
                "documentId": "traj-zxiong-codex-19fd7078bb29d827",
                "eventId": "evt-d777f3c222c490101f884a51437fccbe74b9c7a13b619f3c9cbfb16301a03647",
                "label": "Specificity and source-detection validation results"
              }
            ]
          }
        ],
        "uncertainty": "The audit remained a diagnostic rather than ground truth. Fourteen Gemini human/source judgments were unresolved and excluded without imputation; SimulatorArena profiles were extracted from the same conversations used as references, creating a stated leakage limitation; the full run reduced the smoke design's comparison and order replication; and discrete audit scores limited fine-grained rank inference."
      },
      "insights": [],
      "evidence": {
        "primary": {
          "documentId": "traj-zxiong-codex-c4c6af085497b69d",
          "eventId": "evt-0ccb459c10d9bf3a0ab74b4b225d5e7a62e30162e3905d0177f44ee21c54d573",
          "label": "Direct-audit role, payload, judge, and recoding decisions"
        },
        "supporting": [
          {
            "documentId": "traj-zxiong-codex-19fd7078bb29d827",
            "eventId": "evt-dfd54097cf0339f919ca7db15bd6009eac59d8dabe88e4556832d1bde54f45aa",
            "label": "User-specificity wording request"
          },
          {
            "documentId": "traj-zxiong-codex-19fd7078bb29d827",
            "eventId": "evt-2fbb820162ce668b53d70bd19eed77d6630e5766866f5219a6ec2a62d34f7c18",
            "label": "Order-correction and raw-source question"
          },
          {
            "documentId": "traj-zxiong-codex-c4c6af085497b69d",
            "eventId": "evt-21cc64f198770fe6864bec378e77e8c9120f3c0cd19ad7d8f53499a2ffff21f5",
            "label": "Scope exclusion and score-direction issue"
          },
          {
            "documentId": "traj-zxiong-codex-c4c6af085497b69d",
            "eventId": "evt-2806d6cea139eea5102646d294bf47800e48d381fb578d3d660d177185eff68d",
            "label": "Anonymous order-relative protocol"
          },
          {
