    "chapter": {
      "kind": "validation",
      "title": "A Direct Audit of User Specificity and Human Indistinguishability",
      "overview": "A direct behavioral audit was designed to test whether existing evaluators recover user-specific and human-like behavior, without treating the audit itself as ground truth. Anonymous comparisons, canonical order correction, leakage controls, common-support analysis, and independent judges produced a bounded result: simulators often matched the real response's profile association, but remained readily distinguishable from real users, while evaluator agreement stayed weak and coarse.",
